1
00:00:00.731 --> 00:00:04.270
Welcome to Module Two — neural network
fundamentals. We'll build

2
00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:06.157
the network from the

3
00:00:06.518 --> 00:00:09.973
ground up: the artificial neuron,
activation functions, the forward and

4
00:00:09.973 --> 00:00:13.428
backward pass, and the shape of a Keras
training loop.

5
00:00:14.416 --> 00:00:16.078
It starts with the perceptron

6
00:00:16.434 --> 00:00:20.102
— a single neuron. It weights its inputs,
sums them, adds

7
00:00:20.102 --> 00:00:23.770
a bias, and passes the result through an
activation. On its

8
00:00:24.514 --> 00:00:28.883
own it can only draw a straight line, so
it can't even solve

9
00:00:29.644 --> 00:00:33.043
XOR. But stack neurons into hidden layers,
and a multi-layer

10
00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:36.442
perceptron becomes a universal function
approximator. The activation is what

11
00:00:37.485 --> 00:00:40.313
gives the network its power. Sigmoid and
tanh

12
00:00:40.838 --> 00:00:43.962
saturate and choke the gradient. ReLU is
the cheap,

13
00:00:44.379 --> 00:00:47.864
sparse workhorse you'll reach for by
default, and GELU is

14
00:00:48.231 --> 00:00:51.728
a smoother cousin popular in Transformers.
Pick the activation and

15
00:00:52.408 --> 00:00:56.362
the initializer together — He for ReLU. So
how does it learn?

16
00:00:57.634 --> 00:00:58.804
The forward pass computes

17
00:00:59.556 --> 00:01:03.194
a loss from the inputs and the current
weights. The backward

18
00:01:03.741 --> 00:01:06.537
pass walks the chain rule in reverse,
where each

19
00:01:06.537 --> 00:01:09.333
layer contributes a local Jacobian —
really just

20
00:01:09.912 --> 00:01:11.585
a matrix multiply. The optimizer

21
00:01:12.231 --> 00:01:15.206
then nudges every weight, and GradientTape
makes that whole

22
00:01:15.206 --> 00:01:18.181
tape visible when you need to debug it. In

23
00:01:19.456 --> 00:01:22.400
Keras you'll meet two front doors to the
same

24
00:01:22.888 --> 00:01:26.945
engine. The Sequential API stacks layers
in a line — fastest to

25
00:01:27.455 --> 00:01:29.596
prototype. The Functional API builds
arbitrary graphs

26
00:01:29.596 --> 00:01:31.736
with multiple inputs and outputs. And

27
00:01:32.231 --> 00:01:35.087
subclassing the Model gives you full
research freedom.

28
00:01:36.396 --> 00:01:39.110
One last thing makes deep networks
actually trainable:

29
00:01:39.636 --> 00:01:42.780
normalize your inputs to mean zero and
unit variance,

30
00:01:43.162 --> 00:01:45.743
let batch norm steady the activations in
between,

31
00:01:45.743 --> 00:01:48.324
and match your initializer to your
activation

32
00:01:48.701 --> 00:01:52.828
— because bad initialization is exactly
how gradients vanish or explode. Let's

33
00:01:54.057 --> 00:01:56.640
build your first MLP — open the labs.
